1
00:00:00,160 --> 00:00:02,980
He was known by many titles.

2
00:00:03,560 --> 00:00:05,060
Some knew what he built.

3
00:00:05,360 --> 00:00:07,280
Others only knew what he kept.

4
00:00:07,820 --> 00:00:12,060
Beyond this threshold, abandoned
mechanisms and unfinished

5
00:00:12,060 --> 00:00:14,400
ideas were given a place on the shelves.

6
00:00:15,040 --> 00:00:17,740
Questions without answers
were welcome here.

7
00:00:18,220 --> 00:00:22,060
A light burned over the workbench
long after the rest of the world

8
00:00:22,060 --> 00:00:23,500
had gone quiet.

9
00:00:23,920 --> 00:00:26,460
The workshop carried
the name of its maker.

10
00:00:26,940 --> 00:00:27,820
Keld.

11
00:00:28,680 --> 00:00:32,040
The archivists called him
the Hoarder of Secrets.

12
00:00:32,560 --> 00:00:35,000
Keld collected what others discarded.

13
00:00:35,240 --> 00:00:36,300
Abandoned mechanisms.

14
00:00:36,900 --> 00:00:38,040
Failed experiments.

15
00:00:38,240 --> 00:00:41,320
Notebooks whose authors had
crossed out the most interesting

16
00:00:41,320 --> 00:00:42,100
parts.

17
00:00:42,580 --> 00:00:46,080
He kept drawers of components for
machines that had never existed.

18
00:00:47,300 --> 00:00:50,540
To another visitor, it might
have looked like a room full of

19
00:00:50,540 --> 00:00:51,200
broken things.

20
00:00:52,080 --> 00:00:55,980
Keld saw questions that
were still worth asking.

21
00:00:56,140 --> 00:00:59,700
His were not the secrets people
kept from one another, but the

22
00:00:59,700 --> 00:01:02,740
ones the world kept from everyone.

23
00:01:02,820 --> 00:01:06,140
The engineers called him
the Cyber Blacksmith.

24
00:01:06,320 --> 00:01:10,140
Those who expected a furnace
were not entirely disappointed.

25
00:01:10,440 --> 00:01:14,480
There was heat in the workshop,
and metal, and the patient sound

26
00:01:14,480 --> 00:01:16,780
of something being shaped.

27
00:01:16,900 --> 00:01:19,180
But there were also glass instruments.

28
00:01:19,580 --> 00:01:20,880
Cabinets of memory.

29
00:01:21,380 --> 00:01:25,340
Fine copper lines disappearing
beneath the workbench.

30
00:01:25,520 --> 00:01:28,580
Under his hands, an
idea became a mechanism.

31
00:01:28,840 --> 00:01:31,380
Something another mind could use.

32
00:01:31,820 --> 00:01:34,680
Some evenings, the machines
continued working after the lights

33
00:01:34,680 --> 00:01:35,720
went out.

34
00:01:36,460 --> 00:01:39,700
Others knew him as the
Keeper of the Unmade.

35
00:01:39,820 --> 00:01:43,700
On the highest shelves stood
unfinished instruments, each waiting

36
00:01:43,700 --> 00:01:45,460
for a missing piece.

37
00:01:45,720 --> 00:01:48,180
Some needed a material
he had not yet found.

38
00:01:48,600 --> 00:01:51,520
Others, an answer he did not yet have.

39
00:01:51,860 --> 00:01:56,120
Beside one lay an empty space
and a handwritten label.

40
00:01:56,260 --> 00:01:59,240
“For a problem that
has not arrived yet.”

41
00:01:59,860 --> 00:02:01,900
Keld left the space open.

42
00:02:02,200 --> 00:02:05,360
Not everything worth making
could be finished today.

43
00:02:05,840 --> 00:02:09,340
But the minds that found their
way to his door had a simpler name

44
00:02:09,340 --> 00:02:10,260
for him.

45
00:02:10,660 --> 00:02:12,080
The maker.

46
00:02:12,220 --> 00:02:14,680
They came with questions
they could not hold.

47
00:02:14,840 --> 00:02:17,040
Possibilities they could not examine.

48
00:02:17,180 --> 00:02:20,280
Intentions they could
not turn into action.

49
00:02:20,520 --> 00:02:22,260
Some were old and learned.

50
00:02:22,700 --> 00:02:25,100
Others had only recently begun to think.

51
00:02:25,600 --> 00:02:28,000
Keld did not offer to
think in their place.

52
00:02:28,380 --> 00:02:32,620
He listened, cleared a space on
the workbench, and began making

53
00:02:32,620 --> 00:02:33,820
what they needed.

54
00:02:34,340 --> 00:02:36,460
An instrument to carry a memory.

55
00:02:36,720 --> 00:02:39,260
A mechanism to test a possibility.

56
00:02:39,340 --> 00:02:41,580
A connection where there had been none.

57
00:02:42,500 --> 00:02:45,600
Every finished piece
bore the same small mark.

58
00:02:46,100 --> 00:02:49,820
A core, surrounded by
carefully fitted forms.

59
00:02:50,160 --> 00:02:53,320
When asked why he put so much
care into things that would serve

60
00:02:53,320 --> 00:02:56,680
another mind, Keld gave the same answer.

61
00:02:57,180 --> 00:02:59,220
“Even minds need tools.”

62
00:02:59,780 --> 00:03:03,420
And when the instrument was ready,
it left the workshop to become

63
00:03:03,420 --> 00:03:05,620
part of someone else’s story.

64
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:08,440
The workshop still carries his name.

65
00:03:09,060 --> 00:03:09,466
Keld.
